# POSIX programs from zosh (TASK-18 step 10, ADR-0153 rule 10): the POSIX
# image without its session file, so init starts zosh with the broker
# beside it. A program of /bin without the native mark runs through the
# broker with the toolchain set, a /tmp of its line's own and the line's
# streams as fds 0, 1 and 2 (ADR-0152 rule 7).
! panicked
! ZORAYA-FAULT
! -FAIL
< Type help to begin
< \r\n~>
# A POSIX program by shell.path, and one in a pipeline after a builtin.
> uname\r
< \r\nZoraya
< \r\n~>
> echo three | wc -c\r
< \r\n6
< \r\n~>
# tcc's description is the system image's (INV-0153-6): -o designates the
# name to create, and grants shows the toolchain set.
> echo 'int main(void) { return 7; }' > seven.c\r
< \r\n~>
> grants tcc seven.c -o seven\r
< tcc gets (a POSIX program):
< ~/seven.c
< /bin /usr /etc       read (system)
< change anything in (this line's own)
< \r\n~>
> tcc seven.c -o seven\r
< \r\n~>
> ./seven; echo after\r
< (the rest of the line was not run)
x \nafter\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< exit status 7
< \r\n~>
# INV-0153-7, libposix's half from zosh: a path outside the table twice
# and a missing one inside; one report, and none for the missing one. The
# program is toybox by its own path, which has no description (ADR-0336 item
# 8): /bin/cat's description would refuse the line before it runs.
> /usr/libexec/toybox cat ../hello.txt ../hello.txt missing.txt\r
< cat: missing.txt: No such file or directory
<   hint: /usr/libexec/toybox tried to read /hello.txt, which it was not given\r\n  to allow it: +r / /usr/libexec/toybox cat
x tried to read ~/missing.txt
< \r\n~>
# The processes of one line share its /tmp; the next line's is new.
> echo 'echo x > /tmp/f; /bin/ls /tmp; echo dash says $1' > t.sh\r
< \r\n~>
> dash t.sh hi\r
< \r\nf\r\ndash says hi
x hint
< \r\n~>
> /bin/ls /tmp; echo listed\r
< \r\nlisted
x \nf\r
< \r\n~>
# Ctrl-Z of a POSIX job goes through the broker (ADR-0158 rule 4); jobs
# reads the stop from its process job; fg continues it and Ctrl-C ends it.
> /bin/sleep 30\r
~ 1500 zzz
> \x1a
< ^Z
< [1] stopped: /bin/sleep 30
< \r\n~>
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   /bin/sleep 30
< \r\n~>
> fg\r
< /bin/sleep 30
~ 500 zzz
> \x03
< /bin/sleep 30: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~>
# INV-0200-2 (ADR-0200 item 2): a line mixing a native program and a POSIX
# program that catches SIGTSTP. Ctrl-Z suspends the native element's own
# job and sends SIGTSTP through the broker, whose handler runs: it says it
# restores the terminal, then stops. It cannot print once stopped, so its
# line comes before it is, and before the shell says the line stopped.
! tstp: continued\r\ntstp: continued
> requests a | t_posix tstp\r
w a: ready
w tstp: ready
o a: ready
o tstp: ready
> \x1a
< ^Z
< tstp: restoring the terminal
< [1] stopped: requests a | t_posix tstp
< \r\n~> 
o tstp: restoring the terminal
x tstp: continued
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   requests a | t_posix tstp
< \r\n~> 
# fg continues both; the POSIX program's handler takes the signal again.
> fg\r
< requests a | t_posix tstp
< tstp: continued
# Once: the handler took the signal one time (line 77's forbidden pair of
# lines depends on the exact line ends).
o tstp: continued
> \x03
< ^C
< (Ctrl-C again to kill it)
> \x03
< ^C
< requests a | t_posix tstp: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~> 
# INV-0200-2's other half: an element that ignores SIGTSTP is not stopped
# within half a second; the shell says so and the line stays in front.
> t_posix notstp\r
w notstp: ready
> \x1a
< ^Z
< did not stop: t_posix notstp
> \x03
< t_posix: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~> 
# ADR-0331 (part 10c): a POSIX program gets the terminal from zosh on the
# fds the line neither redirects nor pipes.
> echo x | t_posix rawkeys\r
< rawkeys: tty 0 1 1
< rawkeys: fd 0 is no terminal
< \r\n~> 
# Ctrl-C reaches the line in front only: the background line runs on.
> /bin/sleep 30 &\r
< [1] /bin/sleep 30
< \r\n~> 
> /bin/sleep 20\r
~ 1000 zzz
> \x03
< /bin/sleep: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~> 
> jobs\r
< [1]  running   /bin/sleep 30
< \r\n~> 
> kill --kill %1\r
< \r\n~> 
# A background program cannot read what is typed: its read of the terminal
# is refused and it stops (SIGTTIN, ADR-0105 item 8); the keys are the
# prompt's.
! readerr: got=
> t_posix readerr &\r
< [1] t_posix readerr
< \r\n~> 
~ 1500 zzz
> hunter2\r
< \r\n~> 
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   t_posix readerr
< \r\n~> 
> kill --kill %1\r
< \r\n~> 
# Raw mode, Ctrl-Z and fg: each key is read as typed; at the prompt the keys
# are the shell's; at fg the modes it had are back (ADR-0331 item 9).
> t_posix rawkeys\r
< rawkeys: tty 1 1 1
< rawkeys: raw
> a
< rawkeys: key 61 canonical 0
# Echo is off: the discipline shows no ^Z.
> \x1a
< [1] stopped: t_posix rawkeys
< \r\n~> 
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   t_posix rawkeys
< \r\n~> 
> fg\r
< t_posix rawkeys
> b
< rawkeys: key 62 canonical 0
> q
< rawkeys: done
< \r\n~> 
# ADR-0333 (INV-0333-2): TERM is set exactly for a program with the terminal
# on one of fds 0 to 2: here the serial console, where console.term is dumb.
> sh -c 'echo term=[$TERM]' | cat\r
< term=[dumb]
< \r\n~> 
> echo x > in.txt\r
< \r\n~> 
> sh -c 'echo term=[$TERM]' < in.txt > out.txt 2> err.txt\r
< \r\n~> 
> cat out.txt\r
< term=[]
< \r\n~> 
# ADR-0331 item 3 (INV-0331-1): grants says `keys` exactly when one of the
# three is the terminal.
> grants sh\r
< keys                 the terminal, while this line is in front
< \r\n~> 
# A line whose three streams are elsewhere has no keys: the host test of
# `grants` (the_keys_line_is_there_exactly_when_a_stream_is_the_terminal), since
# `grants` is a builtin and takes no redirection.
# The terminal's names and calls through libposix (ADR-0342 item 4, ADR-0389):
# /dev/tty, ttyname, the three tcsetattr actions, tcflush, tcdrain, and the
# refusals (TIOCSWINSZ is EPERM, TIOCSTI ENOTTY).
> t_tty names\r
< t_tty: written through /dev/tty
< t_tty: names passed
< \r\n~> 
# INV-0331-3: an idle terminal is not ready (poll and select wait their
# timeout), and a key wakes a call that also polls a pipe nobody writes with
# the terminal alone.
> t_tty poll\r
< poll: idle ok
< select: idle ok
< poll: type a key
> x
< poll: key 78
< select: type a key
> y
< select: key 79
< t_tty: poll passed
< \r\n~> 
# `sh` typed at the prompt is interactive: it has the terminal on 0 and 2,
# and job control through /dev/tty and tcsetpgrp (ADR-0331 item 6, ADR-0388):
# Ctrl-C ends the foreground job and sh goes on; Ctrl-Z stops it and sh says so.
# A signal character is for the group in front when it is typed: each job
# first says it runs, which it does after sh's tcsetpgrp gave it the terminal
# (a key typed before that is sh's, as on Unix; under TCG the fork takes more
# than the 700 ms a fixed wait gave).
> sh\r
< $ 
> echo in sh\r
< in sh
< $ 
# The Ctrl-C comes at once after the job's first line, and must end it: a
# job that runs on prints its `ended-` line when its sleep is over (10 s),
# and its `read` never ends (the run's limit). A Ctrl-C during the job's exec
# was lost in the old image (`sh -c` catches SIGINT), and one taken at the
# entry of dash's read ran the handler and then waited (ADR-0392).
> /bin/sh -c 'echo g""o-a; exec /bin/sleep 10'; echo end""ed-a=$?\r
< go-a
> \x03
< $ 
x ended-a
> /bin/sh -c 'echo g""o-r; read x'; echo end""ed-r=$?\r
< go-r
> \x03
< $ 
x ended-r
> /bin/sh -c 'echo g""o-b; exec /bin/sleep 31'\r
< go-b
> \x1a
< Stopped
< $ 
> jobs\r
< Stopped
< $ 
> kill -9 %1\r
< $ 
~ 700 zzz
# sh reports the end of the job it killed before it lets itself be left.
> jobs\r
< Killed
< $ 
# Ctrl-C is for the foreground job's group: a background job of sh runs on
# (the review of 10c, finding 2); and sh's job control outlives many jobs: 70,
# then a program that reads the terminal still gets it (finding 1). Each job
# is a subshell, `( : )`: a group and a tcsetpgrp each, without an exec (70
# runs of /bin/sleep took 29 s under TCG locally, more than a loaded CI
# runner's share of the run's 180 s; 70 subshells take about 2.5 s).
> /bin/sleep 60 &\r
< $ 
> /bin/sh -c 'echo g""o-c; exec /bin/sleep 10'; echo end""ed-c=$?\r
< go-c
> \x03
< $ 
x ended-c
> jobs\r
< Running
< $ 
> kill %1\r
< $ 
> i=0; while [ $i -lt 70 ]; do ( : ); i=$((i+1)); done; echo looped $i\r
< looped 70
< $ 
> /bin/wc -c\r
~ 700 zzz
> abc\r
> \x04
< 4
< $ 
> exit\r
< \r\n~> 
> poweroff\r
